A||||11||Seanh||86GT sluggish in the morning||||9||
Z||000000||Seanh||08-24-2000||04:39 PM||||I have an 86GT 2.8L. I go out in the morning and start it up, and I put it in gear and the RPM's drop to about 400 and I pull it out and back up our driveway and the car doesnt have any power and as much as I push the gas the car just seems to struggle for about the first min or so of my drive. I take off in gear and give it the gas hard and no matter how hard I push it the RPM's dont hardly exceed about 2500. And some times I hear kinda a popping sound out the tail pipes? Sounds to me like it flooding? I am also getting anywhere from 10-14 mpg city. I also hear the side air intake sucking in the mornings. Not that I am implying this only happens in the morning, but also when I let it set for over about 4 hours. I checked the air cleaner and it doesnt seem dirty, but I will replace it anyway. Anyone got any Ideas? <P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Currently upgrading to a NorthStar.||||reg||9
Z||000001||1FST2M6||08-24-2000||04:59 PM||||you'll hear te intake making noise if you have a K&N.. or when the motor is cold cause the IAC valve is pulling LOTS or air through a 9mm hole stick your finger over the hole and you'll have a blood blister and a stalled car. the slugishnessmight be the AIC sticking, the cat being plugged and needing to heat up to flow enought to run decent. have you replaced the fuel filter latley? has the car thrown a code up yet?||||reg||1
Z||000002||Seanh||08-24-2000||06:47 PM||||I did replace the fuel Filter less then a year ago. And no, no codes are present so far. It didnt start doing this until I have the local Pont. service center up a new Distributor in because the old one had somehow gotten bent and sticking and also the pronges were making contact. That car had so much more power then, and I would have left it, if it wasnt dieing and getting 9 mpg. But it was changed and now it is doing this. Tonight Im going to take off that intake hose leading from the air box to the throttle body to see if the body might be gummed up or somthing. The throttle is a Holley Model, is that stock? I didnt change it. Thanks. <BR>Also, did I hear this right? I was told that I should try spraying some carb cleaner in the throttle body? Should I try that?<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Currently upgrading to a NorthStar.<p>[This message has been edited by Seanh (edited 08-24-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Monkeyman||08-25-2000||06:20 AM||||I was gonna suggest a cup of coffee.  That's what I use when I'm feeling sluggish in the morning.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Carrolles||08-25-2000||08:16 AM||||Seanh,<P>Yes you can spray carb cleaner into the throttle body with or without the engine running.  With the engine not running open the butterfly and spray the inside of the throttle body and wipe away whatever gunk you can reach with a rag.  Then start it up and spray some in to let it get drawn into the intake.<P><BR>If you see lots of carbon like buildup it might do you good to use a de-carbonizing kit like the one Ed Parks sells at the Fiero Factory ((205) 420-5391  Fierofactory@juno.com).<P>Have you checked your timing?  Don't assume that the dealer got it right.<P>Did you say that it straightens out after the engine warms up?<P>You might try disconnecting the tach filter also and see if it has any effect.<P>Hope this helps,<P>Carroll<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||fiero56||08-25-2000||09:14 AM||||My '88 Formula has done the same thing for over a year now. After the engine runs for several minutes, performance is fine, but when its cold, there is no power at all, and since it is a stick, I can easily stall it out. I just learned to let it run for a minute before I drive off. Would like to fix it though.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Seanh||08-25-2000||10:40 AM||||I went over to buy some carb cleaner at the store, and I ran across somthing called Throttle Body and Intake cleaner. So I bought that instead. I sprayed it into the Throttle Body as best I could, while running, and then drove it around the block and parked it for the night. This morning, I come out and start it up, it started as it usally does, but when I pulled out of the drive way, it ran normally, with all the power, and it wasnt sluggish. So, I might have found the problem. I will take all the advice and check the timing, and try the tach filter also. I am going to get the decarbonizing kit also, Iv only had the car almost 2 years, its got about 117K miles on it, and the past owner didnt take very good care of it. So, it wouldnt hurt to give that a try too.<P>Thanks<P>BTW, Iv never adjusted the timing myself. How do I do it, or should I let a shop here do it?<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Currently upgrading to a NorthStar.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Seanh||08-25-2000||02:53 PM||||If only coffee came in a spray can!<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Currently upgrading to a NorthStar.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Seanh||08-26-2000||05:32 PM||||Well, the last 2 days, It has ran great in the morning. So I think that the cleaner did it. I am still interested in knowing more about how to do the ignition Time still. Thanks<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Currently upgrading to a NorthStar.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Thor||08-27-2000||12:19 AM||||I also have an 86GT with 188000 miles on her and still running and looking outstanding. Because I'v loved this car so much from day one I'v never been in so much in a hurry as to not let the car run for a minute or two before I start driving. It lets everything warm and lube-up a bit.<BR>  With my experiance with this 2.8 V6 engine<BR>I'm not surprised to see you can fix them with a can of this or that. ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Ralph||08-27-2000||07:00 AM||||Has the possibility of a clogged catalytic converter come up? I have seen the effects of this more than a few times. The ceramic element breaks up and pieces try to get out to the tail pipe and can't. Result is very high back pressure. Have already had this happen on an 87 SE and on an 88 Formula. Both were 2.8's. To troubleshoot, loosen or remove the exhaust pipe ahead of the converter and start the engine. It will be LOUD but you can tell in a moment if that is the problem.....<P>[QUOTE]Originally posted by Seanh:<BR>[B]I have an 86GT 2.8L. I go out in the morning and start it up, and I put it in gear and the RPM's drop to about 400 and I pull it out and back up our driveway and the car doesnt have any power and as much as I push the gas the car just seems to struggle for about the first min or so of my drive. I take off in gear and give it the gas hard and no matter how hard I push it the RPM's dont hardly exceed about 2500. etc.........||||reg||1
Z||000011||Seanh||08-27-2000||11:01 PM||||Ok, Ill give that a try too. If it were clogged though, wouldnt it cause the car to run poorly all the time? Also, My car gets about 120-140 Miles on a tank. I have a lead foot usally, but even though I do, I still think thats pretty bad gas milage. Any Ideas on this. Iv never drove any other Fieros so I dont have much to compare too.<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Currently upgrading to a NorthStar.||||reg||1
